WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:09.300
Let's start with a quick bit of background of how UNIX was implemented before ZOS.

2
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:13.220
The name OpenEdition was used when the support was introduced.

3
00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:16.780
At that time, IBM didn't have the right to call it UNIX.

4
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:22.020
The term OpenEdition is still seen in some documentation, but IBM does now have the

5
00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:28.060
right to the word UNIX after the full UNIX branding was obtained in 1996.

6
00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:35.300
The ZOS UNIX support is now known as UNIX system services and is often called USS.

7
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:40.420
When this UNIX support became available, the role of an MVS system changed.

8
00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:45.540
This opened up new opportunities for MVS, OS390 and now ZOS.

9
00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:50.580
UNIX applications can be ported from other UNIX environments to run on ZOS UNIX system

10
00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:51.580
services.

11
00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:53.900
However, porting might not be trivial.

12
00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:58.340
There's the problem of ASCII and EBCDIC, which must be addressed and we'll get to

13
00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:01.020
that later, believe me.

14
00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:06.540
But first of all, let's see how workstation users can get access to ZOS applications and

15
00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:07.860
data.

16
00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:13.460
With ZOS UNIX, workstation users can access traditional MVS applications and data, as

17
00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:19.580
well as POSIX and XPG 4.2 conforming applications and data on ZOS.

18
00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:25.780
This diagram shows that a workstation application using Motif and XWindows can access data on

19
00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.420
a ZOS system.

20
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:33.540
Motif refers to both a graphical user interface specification and the widget toolkit for building

21
00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:39.740
applications that follow that specification under the XWindows system on UNIX operating

22
00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:40.740
systems.

23
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:46.140
The XWindows system is the underlying graphic system on all modern UNIX systems.

24
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:51.300
XWindows provides the facilities to accept input from a mouse and keyboard and the ability

25
00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.900
to draw graphical objects to a computer screen.

26
00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:58.180
It's supported by all major workstation vendors.

27
00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:04.700
XWindows is a TCPIP application and comes as part of the TCPIP product for ZOS.

28
00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:09.380
The box on the picture that contains the XWindows client in the diagram symbolizes

29
00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:13.900
the XWindows API on ZOS.

30
00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:20.540
Now let's see how high-speed storage devices on Z can be useful for workstations.

31
00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:28.500
The DFSMS-MVS product on ZOS provides data administration functions like backup and archiving

32
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:31.380
of data on a ZOS system.

33
00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:38.780
With ZOS UNIX, DFSMS-MVS can be used to backup and archive the hierarchical file system.

34
00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:43.860
A workstation application might be moved to ZOS if backup is critical.

35
00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:49.020
An alternative solution is to use the Tivoli storage manager product on ZOS which can periodically

36
00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:53.300
upload data on the workstation and archive it on the host.

37
00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:59.020
Either way, folks want to use the same tools and applications to backup and restore individual

38
00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:07.940
ZOS UNIX files residing in a ZOS file system, as they will do with any other ZOS datasets.

39
00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:12.180
Another solution is to backup and restore individual ZOS UNIX files residing in something

40
00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:21.300
called ZFS is integrated into existing DFSMS-HSM backup and recovery and DFSMS-DSS dump and

41
00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:29.430
restore capability, allowing for centralized data management across the ZOS platform.

42
00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:35.950
Now ZOS is well-suited to be a server for batch applications because of its large processing

43
00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:41.030
capability, high-speed printers and high reliability and availability.

44
00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:45.390
Large batch applications can be moved from a workstation to ZOS.

45
00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:52.270
ZOS UNIX shell scripts which are similar to RexxXX can run in the shell session or they

46
00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:55.710
can be submitted as a ZOS batch job.

47
00:03:55.710 --> 00:04:02.190
BPX batch is a utility that you can use to run shell commands or executable files through

48
00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:03.970
the batch facility.

49
00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:09.570
You can invoke BPX batch from a batch job or from the TSLE environment as a command through

50
00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:14.890
the call command or from a C list or a RexxXEC.

51
00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:24.690
So we've seen some of the opportunities and some of the new ways to use ZOS with ZOS UNIX.

52
00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:31.570
Let's look at two exploiters of these facilities which are IBM HTTP server and IBM WebSphere

53
00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:33.970
application server.

54
00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:38.170
Both of these provide a web server environment for ZOS.

55
00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:44.110
This server is a standard compliant product which requires ZOS UNIX and its file system.

56
00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:50.450
It uses the POSIX threading standard, enabling one server to handle multiple client requests.

57
00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:58.430
The homepages stored on ZOS reside in the hierarchical file system or a ZOS-PDS or PDSE.

58
00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:03.310
There are also services and samples provided to show access from a web browser through the

59
00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:08.990
ZOS WebSphere and Liberty servers to Kix and DB2 data.

60
00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:13.950
This is a way to bring your order transaction and processing applications to new users through

61
00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:16.470
the internet.

62
00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:21.750
In the next video, we'll take a look at the components of ZOS.
